\title{D-CH-05 · ∞ first, Segal style, labelled, E1, augmented}
\taxon{Decision}
\meta{id}{D-CH-05}
\meta{area}{charter}
\meta{origin}{design/00-charter.md}
\meta{status}{superseded}
\meta{status-text}{frozen.}
\meta{supersedes}{none}
\meta{superseded-by}{dec-ch-0018}
\tag{decision}
\tag{area:ch}
\tag{status:superseded}

\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{The root is defined at the ∞ level from the start, as a Segal presheaf on a shape category ("style B"): a Kan-complex-valued presheaf on the labelled, augmented shape category \code{Θ^aug_{fc,X}} satisfying a Segal condition (→ D-RT-01). Objects form a \em{set} (labels); vertical morphisms, horizontal morphisms, and cells form spaces. Cells have ordered inputs (E1/planar); there is no symmetric variant at the root. Cells may have empty target (augmented, after Koudenburg).}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{Style B has honest shapes (leveled grids: chains of active maps in Δ, once the shared-sides rule of virtual composition is taken seriously, → \ref{dec-kr-0006}), strict maps as the right maps, and levelwise-combinatorial equivalences; it plugs directly into the Reedy/EZ machinery of the kernel. Labels make Segal conditions over objects into products and remove the need for a completeness condition (Segal-category style, Hirschowitz–Simpson/Bergner). Augmentation makes Yoneda for large objects a structural fact rather than a universe hack (→ D-RT-12).}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rejected}
  \p{Style A (fibrations over \code{N(Δᵒᵖ)}, Gepner–Haugseng generalized non-symmetric ∞-operads): correct, equivalent, but simplicial encoding of tree-shaped data and needs Lurie's fibration toolkit; retained as a comparison target only. Rezk-style unlabelled objects (space of objects, completeness condition). Cubical shapes (no spines, so no Segal conditions; retained for fibrant-object models, → \ref{dec-kr-0009}).}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Note on E1}
  \p{E1-only at the root does not exclude symmetric structures: symmetry enters as a monad \code{T} on the ambient equipment, and \code{T}-monoids are monads in the horizontal Kleisli VDC∞ \code{H-Kl(T, X)} (Cruttwell–Shulman, → \ref{dec-sp-0009}), which is again an E1 object of the root. Symmetric sequences under the composition product are the one-object case. What E1-only excludes is presenting \code{E_n} (\code{n ≥ 2}) as \em{non-symmetric} operads; the braiding is the equivariance. Symmetry is never put into the shapes: cell inputs are paths ordered by composability, and automorphisms in the root shape category would cost elegance everywhere.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{frozen.}
}
